\f0\fs24 \cf2 The lower panels of Giuseppe Bagatti Valsecchi\'92s bed are a masterpiece of delicacy and craftsmanship, created by an anonymous Milanese workshop in the 1880s. Every stitch in the embroidery pays homage to artisanal artistry, and every thread weaves a unique story. The exquisitely refined motifs are based on a watercolor drawing, reproduced with astonishing precision. At the center, a winged figure raises two garlands, while on either side, griffins grasp the festoons with their powerful claws, creating a sense of harmony and movement. But it's not just the elegance of the details that stands out: the composition also draws inspiration from an unidentified Renaissance marble base, documented in photographs preserved in the museum's archive.}